    "domains": {
      "value": "Integer d>=2, real kappa>=2, source indices i=1,...,d; O ranges over the full real O(d), defined by O^T O=I. Both det O=+1 and det O=-1 occur. x is in R^d. Source-wide Gaussian precision class consists of symmetric real A with I<=A<=kappa I.",
      "anchors": [
        "S2.p2",
        "S2.Ex1",
        "S4.SS1.p1.1",
        "S4.E11"
      ]
    },
    "quantifiers": {
      "value": "For every permitted d,kappa and every O in O(d), with deterministic Lambda fixed by d,kappa. Determinant invariance is pointwise. For every pair O1,O2 the corresponding determinants coincide. Haar choice/independence from algorithm randomness describes the hard distribution, not an extra premise required for this algebraic conclusion.",
      "anchors": [
        "S4.SS1.p1",
        "S4.Thmtheorem6",
        "A3.SS4.p3.2"
      ]
    },
    "assumptions": {
      "value": "d>=2 and kappa>=2 are SOURCE conditions of this hard-family slice. Real carriers and finite square shape are TYPING; real orthogonality is SOURCE through O in O(d). Lambda and A are literal definitions, not additional unconstrained objects. Grid bounds, positive definiteness, det O squared=1, determinant-product facts, integrability, Gaussian integration and known normalizer are dependency/results or conclusions, never new public premises. No RULED correction has been supplied.",
      "anchors": [
        "S2.p2",
        "S4.E11",
        "S4.SS1.p1",
        "A3.SS4.p1",
        "A3.Ex160"
      ]
    },
    "conclusion": {
      "value": "Narrow determinant consequence: det(A)=det(Lambda)=product_{i=1}^d lambda_i, hence basis independent. The larger source region states Z_A=(2*pi)^(d/2)*(det A)^(-1/2)=(2*pi)^(d/2)*(det Lambda)^(-1/2), followed by independence from O and knowledge when the spectrum is public. Determinant equality alone neither evaluates an integral nor proves the complete normalizer claim.",
      "anchors": [
        "A3.Ex160",
        "A3.SS4.p3.2",
        "S4.SS1.p2"
      ]
    },
    "scopes_senses": {
      "value": "Determinant is real and unweighted. The standard Gaussian normalizer Z_A corresponds to integral over R^d of exp(-x^T A x/2) against Euclidean Lebesgue volume; the source display does not explicitly print the measure, so this is the Gaussian-normalizer convention inferred from f_A and N(0,A^{-1}), not a verbatim extra source equation. It is not expectation against pi_A or N(0,I), and not a sphere integral. Exponents d/2 and -1/2 have real meaning; natural-number division is not faithful for odd d. Known means determined by public spectrum; it is not a computational or query-complexity bound. The potential is called f_A in Section 4 and V_A in C.4; these denote the same quadratic.",
      "anchors": [
        "S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2",
        "S4.SS1.p1.2",
        "A3.SS4.p2",
        "A3.Ex160",
        "A3.SS4.p3.2"
      ]
    },
    "constant_dependencies": {
      "value": "Exact constants pi, 1/2, kappa and d; Lambda/product determinant depend on d,kappa only. No O dependence; no delta, epsilon, q, sample, or algorithm parameter. In particular kappa<=d^(1-delta) belongs to Theorem 4.1, not to this hard-family determinant/normalizer statement. No unspecified asymptotic constants or large-d threshold apply.",
